  "interpretation": "Sample verified: two terminal fresh-rootfs read-only runs, eight fixed-input calls. The actual +0x258780 helper receives one twelve-byte input and selects raw SHA-1 output. An independent guest-memory execution of the EXISTING vm9_objects digest-reference owner, with fresh relocated ELF pages and explicit fixture allocation services, derives the twenty-byte key from that preimage. Java SHA-1 and hashlib agree with the model. The Python RC4 pipeline ACTUALLY uses this derived key; the observed native key is a comparison-only operand. The resulting stream, generated Protobuf, selected and decoded AES material, native ciphertext and complete prior relation ledger remain exact. The successful pair is run1 and run3; run2 reached only two completed calls at the 420-second deadline and is EXCLUDED. Original and calibrated runners are both fingerprinted and differ ONLY in the 420/1200-second process timer, with the actual runner and deadline recorded separately for each accepted run. Hexadecimal SHA-1, reversed key and a one-bit-changed preimage fail key comparisons per call. The twelve SHA-1 bytes and twelve reduction leaves differ in order, reverse order, multiset AND XOR reduction. Substituting the SHA-1 preimage into the reduction fails to reproduce the Protobuf input. Current source snapshots confirm native data/key constructors copy the observed operands before address reuse. The 263-byte RC4 data is not a direct contiguous slice of the earlier 276-byte decode. Preimage writers reveal four copied bytes and eight appended bytes; their upstream generation is still unknown. No operand bytes, encoded material, keys/IVs, addresses, masks or operand digests are published. Existing source owners are unchanged. The preimage, RC4 data, reduction leaves and lookup key remain explicit runtime inputs; their independent generation, earlier decryption, other inputs, Android equivalence, standalone signer and ordinary Cronet six-header online acceptance remain unverified."
